item.text:
Now I understand the situation. The `Select` component from `@/components/ui/form` wraps a `<select>` and requires a `registration` prop (react-hook-form integration). The `Input` component from `@/components/ui/form` also requires a `registration` prop.

Since the UsersList component manages its own state (not through react-hook-form), I need to find a way to use these components. 

Looking at the `Input` component more carefully:
```tsx
const Input = React.forwardRef<HTMLInputElement, InputProps>(
  ({ className, type, label, error, registration, ...props }, ref) => {
    return (
      <FieldWrapper label={label} error={error}>
        <input
          type={type}
          className={cn(...)}
          ref={ref}
          {...registration}
          {...props}
        />
      </FieldWrapper>
    );
  },
);
```

The `Input` spreads `registration` onto the `<input>` element, so it's designed for react-hook-form. But it also spreads `...props`, which includes `value` and `onChange`. The issue is that `registration` would also spread `name` and other react-hook-form props, which might conflict.

Actually, looking at `InputProps`:
```tsx
export type InputProps = React.InputHTMLAttributes<HTMLInputElement> &
  FieldWrapperPassThroughProps & {
    className?: string;
    registration: Partial<UseFormRegisterReturn>;
  };
